<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Applications of Taylor series</title>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Write out the first few terms of the  Taylor series for
  <m>\sin(2t)</m> and use it to write a series for <m>\frac{\sin(2t)}{t}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use this series to evaluate the limit 
  <md>
  \lim_{t\to0} \frac{\sin(2t)}t.
  </md></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>In the same way, evaluate the limit:
  <md>
  \lim_{x\to0} \frac{e^x - 1  - x}{\cos(2x) - 1}
  </md></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Our goal right now is to find the Taylor series for
  <m>1/(1+x)^2</m>.</p>
        <p>Let's begin with the geometric series:
  <md>
  \frac{1}{1-u} = 1 + u + u^2 + \ldots + u^n + \ldots.
  </md></p>
        <p>Now find the Taylor series for <m>1/(1+x)</m> by substituting <m>u = -x</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>What do you find if you differentiate <m>1/(1+x)</m>?  Use this fact to
  find the Taylor series for <m>1/(1+x)^2</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Here is one way that Taylor series are used in the sciences:
  If you consider an object of mass <m>m</m> that is a distance <m>h</m>
  above the surface of the earth, then the gravitational force is
  <md>
  F = \frac{mgR^2}{(R+h)^2 }=mg\frac{1}{(1+\frac hR)^2}
  </md>
  where <m>R</m> is the radius of the earth.</p>
        <p>Use the series you found above for <m>1/(1+x)^2</m> to write <m>F</m> as a
  series in terms of <m>h/R</m>.</p>
        <p>%</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The constant term should not be unexpected.  Why?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now approximate <m>F</m> by the first two terms in the series.  How
  high above the earth's surface do you need to go before the second
  term changes the approximation <m>F \approx mg</m> by more than 10%?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
